    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d164">

                <pb facs="0297" n="189" xml:id="pg189"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.00 Nanking/57: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul General at Shanghai</hi> (<hi
                        rendition="#italic">Gauss</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of
                        State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Shanghai</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1927-04-11T16:00:00+08:00"><hi rendition="#italic">April 11,
                                1927—4 p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received April 11—7:22
                    a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>Demands on account of Nanking outrages were presented simultaneously at 3:15
                    o’clock this afternoon by the five consuls general to General Pei for immediate
                    transmission to Chiang Kai-shek. No difficulty experienced in presenting
                    demands. Pei made no comment except to say he would transmit the communication
                    to Chiang.</p>
                <p>Legation has been informed.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Gauss</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
